#!/usr/bin/env perl
#
# The regularized incomplete gamma and beta -- pgamma/pchisq/ppois, pbeta/pt/pf/
# pbinom, and the qchisq/qt/qf inverses -- against mpmath, at the width of the
# NV this perl was built with.
#
# Up to 0.3425 both were R's double-precision ports on every build, so on a long
# double or __float128 perl a chi-square or F tail agreed with the truth to about
# 1e-14 however wide the NV; since 0.3426 a LIKER_WIDE_NV build (LikeR.xs) has
# wide replacements for every double-limited piece, and this file is what holds
# them to the NV's epsilon. On a double perl the same cases check the accuracy
# that build has, which is R's from a shape of 1e3 up (see below).
#
# Provenance of every expected value: t/incomplete.gamma.beta.mpmath.py, run as
# `python3 t/incomplete.gamma.beta.mpmath.py cases`, mpmath 1.3.0 at
# mp.dps = 110, printed at 40 digits. They are NOT mpmath's gammainc() or
# betainc() (which raise NoConvergence past a shape of a few thousand): they are
# the defining series and continued fractions, summed at that precision and
# checked against those two functions wherever they converge, and past a gamma
# shape of 1e10 or a beta shape of 1e7 the defining integral, by mpmath's
# quad() at the same precision and checked against the series and fractions
# where both reach (they agreed to 1e-101). The inputs are
# exact binary numbers m * 2**e given as the pair [m, e], so every width builds
# the identical NV and mpmath is fed the identical rational. R's and SciPy's own
# suites pin the same functions only to R's double accuracy (t/distributions.R.scipy.t
# and t/distributions.limits.R.t carry those); nothing there constrains the
# digits past 1e-15, which is the point of this file.
#
# Cases (all in the generated block below):
#
#   @GAMMA  P(a, x) and Q(a, x) and their logs, through pchisq(2x, df = 2a), for
#           a from 1/1024 to 1e10 and x from 60 standard deviations below the mean
#           a to 60 above it, and at fixed points (x < 1, x from 1 to 3, x = 20 a);
#           and a from 1e11 to 1e15 (Temme's expansion), x from 1000 standard
#           deviations below the mean to 1000 above.
#   @BETA   I_x(a, b) and its complement and their logs, through the private
#           Stats::LikeR::_incbeta_xy(), which takes the exact (x, 1 - x) pair that
#           pf() and pt() cannot give it; a = 1/2 and 3/2 against b from 5 to 1e6
#           (the shapes of a t test and a binomial tail), the diagonal up to 1e15,
#           unequal big pairs up to (1e13, 1e9), and a shape of 1/1024 to 5
#           against one of 1e9 to 1e12. Three groups have their own tolerance:
#           "tiny" (a shape of 1/1024), "bup" (where R's bratio() uses TOMS 708's
#           bup() and bgrat(): a smaller shape of at most 1 against a larger one of
#           at most 15 with x0 between 0.1 and 0.29, and both shapes at most 1) and
#           "eps" (a shape of 2^-70 or 2^-130, where it uses apser() or fpser()).
#   @QUANT  qchisq, qt, qf at p from 2**-20 to 0.999, both tails.
#
# What is compared, and the tolerance.
#
# A value is compared in relative terms, the log of a tail by the relative error
# of the log (an absolute error in log P is the relative error in P). The
# allowance is
#
#     tolerance = K * NV_EPSILON * (1 + |log v|)      v < 1/2, or any tail
#               = K * NV_EPSILON                      a log with |v| >= 1/2
#
# because a tail v = exp(-f) computed from a saddle point is good to about f
# epsilons -- the exponent f carries the rounding of the quantities it is
# formed from -- so a value of 1e-1386 is allowed 3200 K epsilons and not K.
# That is the one place the tolerance is not a plain multiple of the epsilon,
# and it is the same on every width: R's own double pgamma() has it too (at
# a = 1e4, x = 7000 the double port is 4e3 epsilons out in absolute terms and
# about 20 in these units).
#
# K was set per width and per case group from the worst error observed over
# the cases below on each of the three builds (double perl 5.44.0, x87 long
# double perl 5.12.5, __float128 perl 5.44.0-quadmath), with a factor of three
# of headroom or more. Observed worst in the tolerance's units (the same figure
# this file prints with diag() on every run), with the previous code's worst on
# the same cases in parentheses where it differed (0.3429's on a double, 0.3428's
# on the wide builds):
#
#                    double                     long double              __float128
#                    worst  (0.3429)   K        worst  (0.3428)  K       worst  (0.3428)  K
#   @GAMMA           23.9              72       2.57   (17.6)    8       4.07   (46.5)    16
#   @BETA            27.7   (73.9)     128      6.54   (38.4)    24      10.1   (3.5e12)  32
#   @BETA, tiny      1.61   (1.2e3)    8        1.07   (64.4)    4       0.93   (38.8)    4
#   @BETA, bup       5.79   (668)      24       1.23   (13.3)    4       1.54   (25.8)    5
#   @BETA, eps       1.34   (1e17)     8        1.43   (1.9e17)  5       0.90   (5.8e31)  4
#   @QUANT           6.67   (36.2)     32       10.2             32      10.2   (14.8)    32
#
# The double column is the same to the last digit with three libms: glibc
# 2.39's, one whose log, log1p, exp, expm1 and lgamma are the long-double
# functions rounded to a double (as near correctly rounded as can be had here),
# and one whose fma() rounds the product to an x87 long double first (as
# Strawberry 5.24's mingw-w64 does), which the module's load-time probe now
# refuses (LikeR.xs, nv_fma_probe()). Its figures are perl 5.44.0's, except
# the "bup" row's 5.79, which is 5.10.1's and 5.18.4's (4.96 on 5.32.1 and
# later): those perls read some of the expected values an ulp off.
#
# Up to 0.3429 a double build kept the Numerical Recipes fraction below a shape
# of 1e3, which is not R, and the "tiny" and "bup" rows were where it was worst;
# the "eps" rows were skipped there, because that fraction cannot hold
# 1/B(a, b) = a for a shape of 2^-70: it was 1e17 epsilons out, and some logs
# NaN. Its "bup" rows also moved with the libm: their complement is 1 - I_x
# with I_x near 1, so one rounding of lgamma() moved it by thousands of
# epsilons. 0.3429's K of 2048 there, measured with glibc 2.39 only, failed
# tests 559-560 (2.5e3 units) on one tester's Solaris, Hurd, aarch64 and x86_64
# Linux machines, whose lgamma() rounds as the long-double one does. Since
# 0.3430 a double build takes R's bratio() dispatch wherever a shape is at most
# 1, and keeps the fraction only for two shapes between 1 and 1e3 (0.3429 had
# left a double build unchanged to the last bit: 81234 incomplete gamma and
# beta values, pt(), qt(), pchisq(), qchisq(), pf() and qf() among them,
# compared between 0.3428 and 0.3429). A wide build takes TOMS 708's
# algorithms for every case since 0.3429. 0.3428's largest wide errors were in
# the continued fractions: the incomplete gamma's for a < 1 and x between 1
# and 3, and bfrac() for a shape of 3/2 against 1e4 and up, each carried one
# rounding per term over 150 to 400 terms; both are now evaluated backward.
# Its 3.5e12 was basym()'s 2/3, a double literal, at unequal shapes past 1e5.
#
# Expected values are parsed by perl's own string-to-NV conversion, which on
# 5.10.1 is unreliable at the exponent extremes (it reads 1e-321 as 0 and 1e307
# five ulp high) and a few ulp out elsewhere; the allowance above is several ulp
# on every width, and a value below 1e-250 on a double perl (1e-4800 on a wide
# one) is not compared at all.

